# Plan prose a safety check can misread

Safety checks read the whole prompt, including plan files. A word that is ordinary in engineering
can be scored as a person or as harm, and the turn stops before any code is read.

## Do not use these in new plan prose, COPY-PASTA lines, or headings

| Avoid | Use instead |
| --- | --- |
| The noun that also means a person seeking office or a job (`candidate`) | **change**, **option**, or **trial** |
| A plan or control that is **dead** | **closed**, **dropped**, or **stuck** |
| A nested view called a **child** | **nested view** or **subview** |

A code change under measurement is a **change**. "Gated change", "change 08", "this change's
premise". Never a person-shaped role.

## Leave real identifiers alone

Do not rename an existing function, file, or error symbol to dodge this rule. When the platform
message itself contains a risky noun, cite the API and the file (`addViewAt` in
`listVirtualization.ts`) and do not paste the message text into a plan.

## Where this applies

`.llm/plans/**`, COPY-PASTA fences, and any prompt the operator will paste. Comments in product
code still follow **comments-future-forward**; this rule is about text an agent will read as the
task.
